now today marks international holocaust  remembrance day the u.n designated  january 27 a day to honor the  anniversary of when allied troops  liberated auschwitz the camp was one of  many set up by nazi germany for the  purpose of mass genocide and forced  labor jewish people were the primary  target of hitler's genocide plan with  six million people killed between 1933  and 1945. his regime also killed five  million others during that same time  period including political enemies  ethnic minorities and those attracted to  the same sex  here at home one holocaust survivor  sharing her experience how she made it  through and christian in studio joining  us with the story and the concern about  the younger generations that's right  brian and  kirsten let me tell you michelle metz is  alive today because she was kept well  hidden during that dark period in time  where unfortunately her father passed  away but it was her mother who later  remarried and that created a path of  opportunities  luck for me that i had my grandparents  luck for me that the nazis didn't find  me the meckie hit us  luck that we came to america and when  you think about the people who survived  it might not have been because of what  they did but it was luck michelle metz  is thankful today for the many blessings  she has as the world honors  international holocaust remembrance day  a dark period in history still relevant  she says and impacting us all whether  it's jews or political prisoners or  black people or native americans or  people who lived in rwanda  it's a very important thing to remember  the 78-year-old sharing these images of  her childhood and reflecting how in the  midst of darkness she was able to  survive this is the anniversary of the  liberation of auschwitz really  significant  because it's focusing on liberation  susan dublin oversees the spurling  cronberg mack holocaust remembrance  center in summerland on the walls are  the faces of some of the survivors in  southern nevada who are slowly passing  on  dublin says less than 100 are still  alive and covet now taking lives  holocaust impacted the entire world most  people don't realize that it wasn't just  germany it wasn't just eastern europe it  was throughout the world it's still  happening maybe not as methodically in  places like rwanda but it's still  happening  well the center also honoring survivors  who have passed away with a garden  memorial the stones there are original  pieces from those roads many families  had to travel back then for things like  medicine and food just to survive check  out eight newsnow dot com for more  details christian gossett is live local  now
 

